1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
Well, I guess that's that maybe your superior intellect is not so superior.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,000
Maybe you don't really know it all. Maybe you're just a regular person.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
And you should participate in the democratic process if you want things to change.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Alright. So, questions about this? Yes?

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:26,000
So, are collective actions, is that a cause for this person, for Whitman?

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Well, Whitman would say, look, democracy is a way of solving collective action problems.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
And I would say that it works great. It's a great system.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
So, you know, because the main thing is when you vote, you're voting for policies for the whole society.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,000
So, Whitman would say, yes, well, you're going to consider the cost and the benefits for, you know,

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
least of all, so he would actually still think of people as voting selfishly.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
But still, like, if everyone can vote, then everyone can represent their interests.

12
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,000
And there's bargaining. Also, never forget the bargaining.

13
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Bargaining is caused by a person's own cost.

14
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
So, bargaining is caused by this. That's why we have markets.

15
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Yes, but we can't bargain over the level of pollution of our society, like with markets.

16
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
You need to have a government with the power to actually say what's going to happen.

17
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Otherwise, then there would be a huge collective action problem.

18
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
If the only way to get pollution down was to go to your neighbors and say,

19
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,000
here's some money, please drive a less polluting car.

20
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Yes?

21
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:33,000
You can trick people?

22
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Trade with people, sorry.

23
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Trade with people, yes.

24
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
So, you said you were going to repeat that?

25
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,000
So, in the sense that you're trading votes.

26
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
You're trading votes or you're trading policies.

27
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:54,000
And so, a lot of the times, it's obviously not as effective or efficient as if you had a currency with which you could actually exchange.

28
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Yes, so Whitman would say, well, I can see the logic of that, buddy.

29
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
And if that's so great an idea, why haven't democracies already done it?

30
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Why hasn't a politician come along and said, you know, the whole problem is that this is all based on trading votes.

31
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Why don't we go and monetize the votes?

32
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:13,000
And, you know, of course, what do normal people say about what would happen if you could sell your vote?

33
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Forget economists.

34
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Just think about what normal human beings would say.

35
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Only rich people rule our country.

36
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:28,000
And they will basically just treat it like their piggy bank or, you know, they'll treat it like a watermelon.

37
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
They'll eat all the fruit and throw away the husk and wear the husk.

38
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,000
So, that's common view.

39
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:34,000
I think we have a saying.

40
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Well, you figured out that's wrong, huh?

41
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Wow.

42
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
All right.

43
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:40,000
He is sarcastic.

44
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:41,000
It's nice to be sarcastic.

45
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,000
You know people like that.

46
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
A nice, sarcastic person.

47
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:46,000
All right.

48
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,000
And now we come to an idea near and dear to my heart, rational rationality.

49
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:53,000
It's the music of the question.

50
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:58,000
But Whitman relies very, very heavily on this rational expectation assumption.

51
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Right?

52
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
So, not that people and voters aren't stupid.

53
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
They satisfy the rational expectation assumption.

54
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:10,000
And remember, the rational expectation assumption is what gives us this result here that the well-informed actually rule the world.

55
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Despite a rule of democracy.

56
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Right?

57
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
But why should we believe the rational expectation assumption is true?

58
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Why?

59
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Who says it's true?

60
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:20,000
What's the evidence that it's true?

61
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:21,000
All right.

62
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:29,000
When I first started working on this idea, my thinking was, well, first let me admit, I never thought there was going to be good evidence for the rational expectation assumption.

63
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,000
But I thought there was going to be better evidence.

64
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Certainly more, like at least a larger stack of evidence.

65
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
When I first started writing the book, I was like, wow, there's almost nothing.

66
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Almost no one has bothered to empirically test this.

67
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
So, very little here.

68
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Of course, it's all behavioral economics, but not normally framed as I'm testing rational expectations.

69
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Only you can think of it that way.

70
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,000
All right.

71
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:01,000
And similarly, I went and tracked down the original articles and found a few arguments, but the arguments were not very good.

72
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:05,000
It seems like almost no one would consider them very good arguments.

73
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,000
One of my research ideas is this.

74
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,000
We're already used to the idea that people weigh the costs and benefits of getting information.

75
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
It's already a well-established idea in economics.

76
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:16,000
All right.

77
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Now, maybe people could weigh the costs and benefits of rationality itself.

78
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Or, in other words, since I was saying you think about rationalities being common sense, maybe common sense could be sensitive to incentives.

79
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Maybe people could have a lot of common sense when there's strong incentives to have common sense and very little common sense when there's weak incentives to have common sense.

80
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
So, that is my idea.

81
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:46,000
So, just as it might be rational to be ignorant, it could also be rational to be irrational.

82
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:47,000
All right.

83
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Now, by the way, if you remember, you've heard of a guy named Jeff Friedman.

84
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
I guess now he works for the Scandin Center.

85
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:58,000
And I published some stuff in his journal, Critical Review.

86
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
He actually has, you know, he philosophically rejects both concepts.

87
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
So, Jeff Friedman literally does think, you know, he said this in credits, he said,

88
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:13,000
rational ignorance is a philosophically unsound concept and you can't use it.

89
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:14,000
All right.

90
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Why not?

91
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Why does philosophy say no one is ever rationally ignorant of anything?

92
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Well, because in order to decide that something is not worth knowing, you would first have to know it.

93
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,000
And you could never, and that can never occur, right?

94
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:34,000
So, you would have to first know it and then say, hey, this is worthwhile, and then not know it anymore.

95
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,000
But like, so it could never be the case that you just say, look, this book isn't worth reading.

96
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
How can you find out if a book is worth reading?

97
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:43,000
By reading it.

98
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Right now, when I ridicule this and say, by this logic, you can never hang up on a telemarketer,

99
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:53,000
you can never delete spam email, you have to go and listen to the whole sales pitch, read the whole thing,

100
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
send the money, because otherwise how do you know that it is a waste of your money?

101
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Until you've heard the whole thing.

102
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Right?

103
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:09,000
And Jeff was not very happy about this, which in my mind is a, you know, a knockdown reduction ad absurdum of what he's saying.

104
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:14,000
And then, of course, you know, so then you have similar arguments about rational rationality,

105
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,000
saying no one ever rationally decides to not be rational.

106
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
How do you know?

107
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Many people do.

108
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Right?

109
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:29,000
Again, especially if you just think about it as how, what level of common sense do you want to have today on this issue?

110
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:36,000
Do you want to have the high level of common sense, or do you want to have the low level of common sense?

111
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
All right.

112
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
In other words, what I say is you think about irrationality as being a normal good.

113
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Now, the constant normal reaction to this is, well, all right, fine, you could model that way,

114
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:50,000
but why would anyone want this dubious good of irrationality?

115
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
And here's my main story.

116
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:59,000
Well, if you are a totally Vulcan rational, totally Vulcan, totally rational person,

117
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:05,000
then you risk having to change your mind any time new evidence comes along.

118
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
And for most people this works.

119
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:11,000
Most people have a list of sacred beliefs that can change their lives,

120
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:17,000
and if you're totally calm and open to argument, you might have to switch your belief very drastically

121
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,000
just because someone has better arguments than you expected they would have.

122
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:22,000
All right.

123
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:27,000
So, anyway, see, the big reason why you would want to be irrational is you bribe the judge.

124
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,000
Right?

125
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:34,000
If you are irrational, then you don't have to worry you're going to lose any of the beliefs that give meaning to your life

126
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:39,000
because the judge is corrupt, namely the judge is your own mind.

127
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:44,000
So you aren't going to go and evaluate your own beliefs as a Martian would or as a Vulcan would.

128
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
Instead, you evaluate it like your best friend would.

129
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:53,000
Like if you were accused of murder and you say, well, I'm also the judge, I didn't do it, and I know that.

130
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:57,000
I rule as a matter of law that I'm innocent.

131
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:03,000
So, in general, so people derive comfort, security, and a sense of identity from their belief structure.

132
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:07,000
So, of course, traditionally it was religion.

133
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Traditionally it was religion.

134
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:13,000
So, again, like throughout most human history and around the world today, if you want to go and make friends,

135
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
do you go up to someone and say, what's your religion?

136
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
And then go, well, that's stupid.

137
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:18,000
Wrong.

138
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
Ridiculous.

139
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:21,000
Right?

140
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
That's not a good way to make friends anywhere.

141
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:24,000
Right?

142
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:25,000
And why?

143
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:27,000
I mean, you might think that they would then say, really?

144
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:30,000
It's wrong?

145
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
I've always thought animism was the true religion, but it's not.

146
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,000
Or Zoroastrianism.

147
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:35,000
Why do you like saying that?

148
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:36,000
You say, no, actually it's not.

149
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:40,000
Turns out the real religion is Methodism.

150
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
And here's why.

151
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,000
Wow, thanks.

152
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:48,000
This is not a conversation that occurs very often on Earth.

153
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:49,000
Right?

154
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:50,000
And why?

155
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:55,000
Because religion gives people the sense of comfort, security, identity.

156
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
So, I was just at a funeral.

157
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
I heard a lot of this stuff.

158
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
And I didn't want to make people cry.

159
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:04,000
So, I did not say, that sounds made up.

160
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:05,000
Right?

161
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
Yeah.

162
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:14,000
And even when it's stuff that sounds really made up, like, we believe the soul goes to heaven after 40 days.

163
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
Immediately.

164
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,000
About 39 days, 40 days.

165
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Right?

166
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:24,000
Sorry for your loss.

167
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
It's the better thing to say.

168
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Rather than saying, how do you know this?

169
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:30,000
Right?

170
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:31,000
And you say, it's in the Bible.

171
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:32,000
It's not in the Bible.

172
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:33,000
Think about that.

173
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
The Bible says, Jesus spent 40 days on Earth.

174
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
Doesn't say everybody does.

175
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:41,000
And Jesus spent 40 days on the other end of the Bible in a body.

176
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
So, this is completely wrong.

177
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
It's based no basis in anything.

178
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:50,000
But, don't say that unless you want to make everybody cry.

179
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:54,000
Which when I was a teenager I was a bit, but now I'm not.

180
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
All right.

181
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
So, this is a big reason.

182
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:04,000
So, when I was writing the book, one of my favorite books that I came across is a book called The God That Failed.

183
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:08,000
This is a book which is a collection of autobiographies of former communists.

184
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:11,000
Actually, former Stalinists, specifically.

185
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:12,000
All right.

186
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:16,000
So, it's like 15 biographies, or autobiographies, autobiographies.

187
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:23,000
And not one of the autobiographies did someone say, I was really happy to learn that Stalin was actually not a great guy.

188
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:31,000
I was really happy to learn that something other than communism, the answer is, I'm a curious person and I always like to learn new things.

189
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
No one said anything like that.

190
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:37,000
Instead it was one tale of woe and misery after another.

191
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
Like, I just wanted to kill myself.

192
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,000
Or like Arthur Kessler, who went on to be a famous novelist.

193
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:46,000
He said, it was like giving up Stalin was like emotional harakiri.

194
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:50,000
It was like taking a sword and stabbing it into your own belly and stirring it around.

195
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,000
That's how bad it felt to stop believing in Stalinists.

196
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
These are normal human reactions.

197
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
It's like, look, these beliefs matter to me.

198
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
They're what gives me my identity.

199
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
You attack them, you attack me as a human being.

200
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:07,000
This isn't just some neutral search for truth.

201
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:12,000
It is, oh yeah, so by the way, I did it again.

202
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:16,000
I accidentally switched from religion to politics without mentioning the transition.

203
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,000
But yeah, it's a very easy transition.

204
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:24,000
So common observation is politics replaced religion in the 20th century.

205
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:28,000
The kind of person who would have been a priest became a demagogue in the 20th century.

206
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:29,000
Yes?

207
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:34,000
What do you think about Jason Brennan's thing on Hobbit's rule against the Vulcans?

208
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Yeah, it's all fits.

209
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:44,000
But it's not even just that you stick to your beliefs, you stick to straw man ideas about the other side.

210
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:45,000
Yeah, of course.

211
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:51,000
Yeah, well, that's one of the easiest ways to stick to your beliefs is to think everyone disagrees with you as an idiot.

212
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
Right.

213
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:54,000
Yes?

214
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,000
This is kind of specific to the book, but wouldn't it be more comforting if you were a communist

215
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:05,000
to find out that all the problems you observed were just because of this one weirdo who somehow was in charge of what's like,

216
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:09,000
when Khrushchev says actually all these things were Stalin's fault and he's dead.

217
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:14,000
Well, remember, these are people who changed their mind when Stalin was still in power.

218
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
So that's the key.

219
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:24,000
So once I actually got an email from a communist, I did a lot of work in grad school on the atrocities of communism.

220
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
He said, look, fine, there were a lot of atrocities.

221
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:34,000
Those all fixed in 1956 when Khrushchev went and said, we're really, really sorry to some of you.

222
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,000
Especially people who were in the Communist Party.

223
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:42,000
That was the real tragedy is when Communist Party members suffered.

224
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:46,000
That's the worst thing that could happen is for a loyal communist to suffer, right?

225
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:47,000
Right?

226
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:48,000
All right.

227
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:56,000
Now, something else you can pile on is to say, look, rational thinking is often hard, painful, discouraging work.

228
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:01,000
So you ever work on a math problem for an hour and you get it wrong a bunch of times and finally you get something that kind of looks like the right answer?

229
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
Then you go, yep, that's the answer.

230
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:04,000
I'm done.

231
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:05,000
Right?

232
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:08,000
Now, reasonably, you have to say, well, given that I messed it up a bunch of other times,

233
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:13,000
maybe I worked on this for one more hour, I would find that this kind of answer is wrong, too.

234
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:20,000
But it's just so discouraging to do this kind of thing that a lot of people would just preemptively say, I've got it.

235
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:21,000
Solved.

236
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:22,000
Recognition accomplished.

237
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:25,000
I've got the answer.

238
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:30,000
Just the whole mindset of what you have to do in order to become accurate.

239
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
So, again, I talk a lot about Phil Tetlock.

240
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:38,000
He's someone who actually has a lot of great empirical work on what are things you do if you want to become accurate.

241
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:41,000
A lot of it is pretty simple.

242
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
Again, most of it is common sense.

243
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:47,000
Don't say things are impossible or certain unless they're like 1 plus 1 equals 2.

244
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:50,000
So for any interesting question, stop saying it's certain or impossible.

245
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
All right?

246
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
And here's another one.

247
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:58,000
Say what would count as you being right or wrong before the events pass.

248
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:03,000
Try to be as unambiguous as possible.

249
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:09,000
So you then aren't tempted to go and wait for whatever happens and say, oh, just as I predicted, once again.

250
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
All right?

251
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
So you didn't say what would count as winning.

252
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:17,000
You waited to see what happened and then did what Tetlock calls self-scoring.

253
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:24,000
Self-scoring where you go and referee your own match and say, wow, I win.

254
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:25,000
I'm the winner.

255
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:26,000
I'm also the player.

256
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
But I'm the player, I'm the referee, and I'm the winner.

257
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:33,000
Wow, that's amazing that you won when you are the referee.

258
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:34,000
Big surprise.

259
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
So what is a good thing to do?

260
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:41,000
Well, minimum, like publicly say, if the unemployment rate goes above 8.5 percent, then I am wrong.

261
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:44,000
Otherwise, I am right.

262
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:50,000
Even that's not as good as actually betting with someone who disagrees with you because when you go and say that 8.5,

263
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:55,000
it may make the goalposts so easy for yourself to pass.

264
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:02,000
So I have a Keynesian friend who said, look, the fact that inflation stayed low during the last recession shows Keynesianism is right.

265
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:08,000
And I said, yeah, but I'm not, at least you're kind of Keynesian and I predicted exactly the same thing.

266
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
So how does that show you're right?

267
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:15,000
Again, the right thing for him to do, what it would have been to have done, would have been to find a person that he disagreed with

268
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
and work out a list of disagreements.

269
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:25,000
And then say, we have hammered out a deal where two people, both of whom noticeably disagree,

270
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:29,000
have worked out a list under which only one will be right in the end.

271
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:33,000
And we are now publicizing for credibility.

272
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:37,000
Of course, there's a big downside to doing that kind of thing.

273
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:40,000
Egg on your face.

274
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:43,000
If you do anything that would allow you to be unambiguously right,

275
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:47,000
it will also automatically allow you to be unambiguously wrong.

276
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
People don't like that.

277
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:54,000
And then, again, very basic things like don't be a single theory to explain everything person.

278
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:57,000
Admit that there's a lot of theories, all of which have sub-use.

279
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:01,000
There's a bunch of words that you can just drop from your vocabulary if you want to get better.

280
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
Drop the word inherently.

281
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:06,000
Drop the word necessarily.

282
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,000
These are terrible words if you want to go and predict anything.

283
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:13,000
But remember, as you were saying, that necessarily, look, I don't say it's necessary.

284
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
I say it's going to happen.

285
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
I don't say it's necessary.

286
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:20,000
I don't know of any way of empirically checking whether something's necessary.

287
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:23,000
So let's not bet on that.

288
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:26,000
Let's bet on something that we can see.

289
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:29,000
And then, an indirect reason is also important.

290
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:32,000
The way that other people treat you may depend upon your beliefs.

291
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:35,000
So right now, suppose that you were to announce that you know that you disagree

292
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
with all of your current controversial political beliefs.

293
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
How many friends would you lose?

294
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:45,000
You go on Facebook and you say, I used to believe the following ten things.

295
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:48,000
I now believe none of them.

296
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
And first, most people usually think it's a joke, right?

297
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
Check to see if it's April Fool's.

298
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
And then you go and say, look, and here's why.

299
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,000
How many friends are you likely to lose?

300
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Of course, no one here would have kind of low friends that would get mad at you just for

301
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
irrationally updating reviews.

302
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:08,000
But other people have bad friends that will get angry just because they change their minds

303
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:13,000
and won't give you the benefit of the doubt and won't say, since we are friends, I know

304
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:17,000
you're well-meaning and a reasonable person, so please share your arguments with me.

305
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:20,000
But a lot of people don't have friends like that.

306
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:25,000
They're stuck with regular human friends.

307
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
Now, what is the price of rationality?

308
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
So at first glance, it may be hard.

309
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:32,000
But then you realize a lot of things in economics, we talk about the price,

310
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:35,000
there is an invisible price like the price of safety on the job.

311
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:38,000
You don't actually go to your employer and say, I'm going to pay you something to go

312
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
and make the job safer.

313
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:46,000
Instead, what do you actually do in the labor market for what workers actually do?

314
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:54,000
Like how do we actually work out safety, regulation aside, but besides that, how do we work it out?

315
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:55,000
Tacitly.

316
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:56,000
Work it out tacitly.

317
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
Yes, so, yes, you have compensating differentials.

318
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:02,000
So employers know, look, if the job is dangerous, I'm going to need to offer more money.

319
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:05,000
I can go and make it safer, and then I can offer less money.

320
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:10,000
Or similarly, workers could say, look, I'll work here, like I'll work under these conditions,

321
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:14,000
so I'm not going to work under those conditions, and I'm not going to have to pay any more.

322
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:15,000
All right.

323
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:18,000
So same way you think about the price of rationality.

324
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
Here is a price.

325
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:26,000
We think about it as the dollar value of the bad material consequences you have as a result

326
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:37,000
of being irrational, and the quantity, the amount of deviation from rational expectations,

327
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:41,000
and the spa-regular old demand curve.

328
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:45,000
So when the price of rationality is high, which it often is, like if you're a doctor

329
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:49,000
and you believe I'm such a great doctor, I'm better drunk than other doctors are sober,

330
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
costly belief, right?

331
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:52,000
Like they destroy your career.

332
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:57,000
So the price is high, and certain people at least consume less, maybe not at all.

333
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
I think I mentioned betting to you guys.

334
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:02,000
I'll mention it again, because it can't be uptension, on or not.

335
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:05,000
There are so many people who say ridiculous things, and then you challenge them to a bet,

336
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:10,000
where if what they were saying were genuinely true, they should want the bet.

337
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:14,000
Someone says, it is definitely true that Hillary will win the 2008 election.

338
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:15,000
There's some problem with that.

339
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:16,000
I've never said this.

340
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:17,000
You disagree.

341
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:20,000
You're just a fool, because you don't understand that Clintons will not allow themselves to lose.

342
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
The end.

343
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:27,000
If you really believe this all the way down, if someone said, fine, give me 1,000 odd odds,

344
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:32,000
you would say, sucker, sucker, I'm going to take all your money, right?

345
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:36,000
And you would say, fine, I'll go and bet you my house against $1,000.

346
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:37,000
How's that?

347
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:42,000
But in fact, of course, almost no one goes and takes these bets, which you might say

348
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
just shows they were lying.

349
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:49,000
They seem out there to be pretty good actors to be lying when they are making these grandiose claims.

350
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:53,000
Instead, they are letting their emotions run away with them.

351
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:54,000
Right?

352
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:57,000
They're saying, there's no way that's possibly going to happen.

353
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:25,000
Does he believe it?


